 # GitHub Actions Security Review
 
+## Toolchain integration
+
+Read [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md) and [the Actions guide](../../../docs/GITHUB_ACTIONS.md) before applying the upstream workflow below.
+This skill is read-only. Do not push, open pull requests, trigger workflows, or send payloads to test a finding. A PoC is a written sketch only.
+The upstream threat model and the "do not flag" rules decide what is an exploitable finding.
+The Toolchain rules are stricter. Report a deviation from them in a separate "Policy deviations" section, even when no external attacker can exploit it:
+
+- An action that is not pinned to a full commit SHA with a version comment, including first-party `actions/*` and `github/*`.
+- A job token permission that the job does not need, or a checkout without `persist-credentials: false`.
+- An input `${{ }}` expression in `run` text, including `workflow_dispatch` inputs.
+- A publication token or secret in the repository or workflows.
+- `app` choices in `test.yml`, `build.yml`, and `release.yml` that do not match `apps/`.
+
+Write the report in Russian. A human decides how to handle each finding.
+
+## Upstream workflow
+
 Find exploitable vulnerabilities in GitHub Actions workflows. Every finding MUST include a concrete exploitation scenario — if you can't build the attack, don't report it.
 
 This skill encodes attack patterns from real GitHub Actions exploits — not generic CI/CD theory.
